{
  "id": "proofs.divisibility.product-of-divisors",
  "course": "proofs",
  "topic": "divisibility",
  "kind": "counterexample",
  "title": "Disprove: a | c and b | c mean ab | c",
  "prompt": "Disprove: for all positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, if $a \\mid c$ and $b \\mid c$, then $ab \\mid c$. Give a counterexample.",
  "answer_format": "Integer values for every variable, like a = 4, b = 2, c = 2.",
  "difficulty": 1,
  "tags": [
    "counterexample",
    "divisibility"
  ],
  "data": {
    "variables": {
      "a": "positive",
      "b": "positive",
      "c": "positive"
    },
    "hypothesis": {
      "op": "and",
      "args": [
        {
          "op": "divides",
          "args": [
            "a",
            "c"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "divides",
          "args": [
            "b",
            "c"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "conclusion": {
      "op": "divides",
      "args": [
        "a b",
        "c"
      ]
    }
  },
  "answer": "a = 2, b = 2, c = 2",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "What goes wrong when a and b share a factor?"
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Take $a = b = c = 2$: $2 \\mid 2$ twice, but $ab = 4 \\nmid 2$.",
    "So the statement is false. (It holds when $a$ and $b$ have no common factor.)"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "hammack-bop3",
    "section": "9.1 Counterexamples",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
